answer/value "images/8c/8c2cca8fc22c2d88ab2cf94205c9058a1f9bc3fffc5e26c223b3fed60e04057d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392613} {:answer/id #uuid "d603ba90-00d7-52b8-a737-e0ff062b4032" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0a/0a31db89be8ebaff6fdcb362fa80c26560e6ea94b0794f0a655bd066e00d36c3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d31f274b-5b4d-5dce-b257-d19c24fd9b76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fd/fdd6b61f7bec06e6e6d95881ee0b0a11eb0ef490c6b2f242e0181c58ae6b990e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392611} :answer-field/id #uuid "540d3f99-dea4-52b1-a147-cd36aac0c02b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392609}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c05827a9-fdcf-58d8-bfea-b9a09196b4ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-48081" :question/problem "Select the function that is continuous at $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is shown below.  Indeed, the graph can be drawn in a neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nAll the other given graphs contain breaks at $x=2$ and it would be impossible to draw them in a neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking the pencil off of the paper."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233061 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "62ec00bb-a7dc-5ae8-ae64-a716f57687a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-102106" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d9cf9a5c61cbf1796cf6682b129b4c05171c364c7b34e7cce176ba27e9c9cb24.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nI. - $x=-1$\nII. - $x=2$\nIII. - $x=4$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=-1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=4.$ Since there is an asymptote at $x=4,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=4$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is  \"I and II only\".\n\n![](images/95/95967eb82512aae261936a6c09e2f474c72c9a130d3ecb527f62408822d88c96.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234403 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b9857052-b60b-5b90-a22b-cd84e32dabbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-78133" :question/problem "![](images/43/43544a1ba403055094cae54fed9772df353d3697f50888728ebee61e77b729da.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nI. - $x=-7$\nII. - $x=-4$\nIII. - $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-7.$ Because there is a jump at $x=-7,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-7$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-4.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=-4$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-1.$ Since there is a break at $x=-1,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is: \"II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237871 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "205e1e30-bd9c-50eb-9cb8-7a0cfa8d844e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236026} {:answer/id #uuid "55075645-dbcf-5f0e-b2df-212debdec0a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236360} {:answer/id #uuid "ba9d5b00-dd0c-5d54-892b-57dbbaf60d52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237010} {:answer/id #uuid "c1212b72-b517-5a23-a3ee-15d5d71a8557" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237043} {:answer/id #uuid "fac8b27f-38bb-5d09-bf82-a4782974c9da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba9d5b00-dd0c-5d54-892b-57dbbaf60d52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237010} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9b2dffa-7952-59b0-931a-25debad78721" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237700}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a3b92036-278a-5fb6-a1d2-39caf6b31601" :question/math-academy-id "q-102115" :question/problem "![](images/d1/d1d91b2d791d69c512a6bd2e7d523563de076a7f3afda53513234e799152ef8d.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\n- $x=-1$\n- $x=1$\n- $x=3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-1.$ Since there is a jump at $x=-1,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1.$ Since there is a break at $x=1,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=3.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=3$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is  \"III only\".\n\n \n\n![](images/57/57415045f7a1cc1c896756ff19cb17f90074151a25d12c56068fb6f86a064753.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336004 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68f016cd-f1df-5564-b277-b9ece07b1add" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/42/42e1d99095fd174c1e9eb1835cf0a26f16d58ce9392a7654ad0fd0526fd35add.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333951} {:answer/id #uuid "6f9f44b0-368f-559a-b2a5-34548d645434" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d1/d124370f929cdd5f42cabe0f2588e668fb90a8e8abbd2140fe9db33d3e3b2fbf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334006} {:answer/id #uuid "e6fbc9e1-c840-5d83-b28b-d761b9044e3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/17/17fc3c79ab7a2e3e6d724821e9e43e66f35881d068b68a04332aa028dc51bd1a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335006} {:answer/id #uuid "ebc73aff-3ed2-576e-8a12-52412bd890b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/76/76a1b2ca2dd29f0689693b1ed347c3e7f2c4359b2760f5934f661643eb6a5a64.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335053} {:answer/id #uuid "f379af21-00b7-5d16-a491-ec53069d2b3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/13/135973e3013842e2313f678d8538288a7f6bf7612ea30bf4e69791a1efcd559a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335123}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f9f44b0-368f-559a-b2a5-34548d645434" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d1/d124370f929cdd5f42cabe0f2588e668fb90a8e8abbd2140fe9db33d3e3b2fbf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334006} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f84de5c-4838-586d-b50e-95004b3f22d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335536}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e470e82c-c1d4-5e27-9c41-1a58d40dcb44" :question/math-academy-id "q-69782" :question/problem "Select the function that is continuous at $x=5.$" :question/worked-solution "Of the given options, the only one where we can draw the graph of the function without taking our pencil off of the paper in a neighborhood of $x=5$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/d8/d8720531a6a06d2192ff76b04c9038e6029fa2c8844e5315d416dbbe11f38cc5.png)\n\nAll other given options have breaks or asymptotes at $x=5$, so it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=5$ without taking our pencil off of the paper."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348065 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2049f5f7-1d2c-5379-b695-8528aba89f66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345636} {:answer/id #uuid "aacbc7a0-b134-5a75-a87d-fd53948b0296" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346843} {:answer/id #uuid "bb9db866-8720-598b-af83-114dd5773144" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346978} {:answer/id #uuid "dd80fc1e-6eab-5ddb-9563-26cba2c2d2a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347298} {:answer/id #uuid "f22c335d-cda9-5ae9-995c-087b8ca8dae1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347493}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aacbc7a0-b134-5a75-a87d-fd53948b0296" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346843} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b5d2966-8562-5e0a-b5f1-e9381bdd7052" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347878}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ac549bb-41f9-5150-895e-d407a77f8e37" :question/math-academy-id "q-78131" :question/problem "![](images/3c/3c0dc4b8f7f45a5bc1177778431eccf9bcaac1bf6d520d605f9e6bc08d5b1d91.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nI. $x=-2$\n\nII. $x=0$\n\nIII. $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-2.$ Since there is an asymptote at $x=-2,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348066 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01aeaace-3dee-5861-928d-6a23090d00f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345366} {:answer/id #uuid "01c007c5-b958-5f05-bcdb-d16eb849a4b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345367} {:answer/id #uuid "02100b32-fbcc-582d-903e-bf23af1e8c8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345368} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a6767c-d2f1-526e-934e-fbbf34e09777" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347068} {:answer/id #uuid "f40cbba4-ff28-567c-bde7-f1a1ff700f2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347512}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f40cbba4-ff28-567c-bde7-f1a1ff700f2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347512} :answer-field/id #uuid "01de2c48-cbc5-5a34-8784-842c895031e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347609}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c2c6cc4-6d0f-5ca4-bd69-989208f699b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-78132" :question/problem "![](images/89/890e7eaf519a65ca19d4a90b604e7d7a8ab7054635296c4b08f000546f23b545.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nI. $x=-2$\n\nII. $x=1$\n\nIII. $x=3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $-2.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=-2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1.$ Since there is a jump at $x=1,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- Therefore, the function $f(x)$ is continuous $x=3.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of  $x=3$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is  \"I and III only\".\n\n![](images/7c/7c96c401ddae0049859fd5608cb08c6ce9ae8a2e6c3db69d7d4b379b3824094e.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392615 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5cffd7b-e09a-5e8f-9bc6-d30e9bf80623" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c6/c6f0914f0b218164bc99fdefe7d83dffe4591a6b704e2243013d05c2b117ef17.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392617} {:answer/id #uuid "3ba0ee0f-2ced-5835-84e5-0ed53e1549d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c2/c2f85d1a2becc7bfe6f71d7f6fcb178f4707f4b41956015eabb38610de95feca.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392618} {:answer/id #uuid "d5853982-4adc-5148-b192-1f951c92f732" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/14/14349b3dee80a6d451efedc55b91b5e709139f1a93fabf539d605843513f6141.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392619} {:answer/id #uuid "a3c69f46-c3ba-5974-ae7f-6acba3a9675b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/30/3073f651e0194c8675629f8fc52d45694a64680e3208b8fd7ef066e38d715033.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392620} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c4e630-276e-54fd-922f-6f7389462a61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c9/c95d9944eb569c36d0b546257b5fde5a2d8c2aa8984eb98d553df832c2b1989f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5cffd7b-e09a-5e8f-9bc6-d30e9bf80623" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c6/c6f0914f0b218164bc99fdefe7d83dffe4591a6b704e2243013d05c2b117ef17.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392617} :answer-field/id #uuid "9780e3c9-7665-5af4-99ca-1678d1cb66a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392616}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b614a15-6e39-5d82-8606-db6fffa56cff" :question/math-academy-id "q-48082" :question/problem "Select the function that is continuous at $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is shown below.  Indeed, despite the fact that the function has a discontinuity at $x=2$, the graph can be drawn in a neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n![](images/90/90c2fbe99d0b202e09244084d2501608018e030e6660a225f708cbc6b462dccd.png)\n\n   \n\nAll the other given graphs contain breaks at $x=0$ and it would be impossible to draw them in a neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking the pencil off of the paper."}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is Continuous at a Given Point"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068696 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068548 :question/id #uuid "514240a0-8a38-564f-ab4c-d0bacb0c5f6b" :question/math-academy-id "e-3568" :question/problem "What are the points of discontinuity of the function $y=g(x),$ plotted below?\n\n![](images/6d/6d0d3c2cd072841546e66d70105436200e316e156901f8fe27dc150e96ff3da4.png)" :question/worked-solution "The points of discontinuity are at $x=0,$ $x=1,$ and $x=2.5.$ It is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of these three points without taking our pencil off of the page."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31eacf5a-75a5-5b38-8c24-4b1598ecf3a7" :knowledge-point/que